# The Linux collector has to reach the target with LF line endings. A CRLF shebang
# fails with a "bad interpreter" error that reads like the file is missing, so the
# collector checks for CRLF and refuses to run rather than fail confusingly. Forcing
# eol=lf here keeps a Windows clone from reintroducing the problem whatever the
# machine's core.autocrlf is set to.
#
# Invoke-EvidenceCollection.ps1 does not rely on this: it strips CR bytes from its own
# copy before uploading, so a collector that reached this machine some other way (a
# zip, an email, a text-mode transfer) still works. The attribute keeps the repository
# itself correct; the upload path makes it not matter.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-side files keep native line endings.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.vbs text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.txt text eol=crlf
*.md  text eol=crlf
